The function `balanced_brace_block` in `src/reparse.rs` is a Rust
reimplementation of `SyntaxBuilderUtil.checkBraceBalance` /
`hasProperBraceBalance`, called by `JavaCodeBlockParser.canBeReparsedIncrementally`
in JetBrains intellij-community at commit
840d3be11dfdeada533d05052008b48816b6cc4e:

https://github.com/JetBrains/intellij-community/blob/840d3be11dfdeada533d05052008b48816b6cc4e/platform/syntax/syntax-util/src/com/intellij/platform/syntax/util/parser/SyntaxBuilderUtil.kt
https://github.com/JetBrains/intellij-community/blob/840d3be11dfdeada533d05052008b48816b6cc4e/java/java-syntax/src/com/intellij/java/syntax/element/lazyParser/JavaCodeBlockParser.kt

Those two files state: "Copyright 2000-2025 JetBrains s.r.o. and contributors.
Use of this source code is governed by the Apache 2.0 license."
That file header is the license for this derivative, not the separate
"JetBrains Open-Source Build Terms" document at the root of
intellij-community (LICENSE.txt there), which governs the IDE product build.

`Parser::lambda_decision` follows the commit/rollback rule of
`PrattExpressionParser.parseLambdaAfterParenth` in the same tree: a
parenthesized parameter list is a lambda when `->` follows it, and is not
parsed as one otherwise. That function is Apache-2.0 under the same file
header. The Rust function is a reimplementation of that decision, not a copy
of the Pratt parser.

https://github.com/JetBrains/intellij-community/blob/840d3be11dfdeada533d05052008b48816b6cc4e/java/java-syntax/src/com/intellij/java/syntax/parser/PrattExpressionParser.kt

`Parser::recover_stmt` follows `StatementParser.parseStatements` in the same
tree: when the current token does not start a statement, one token becomes an
error and parsing continues. That stops a run of bad tokens from consuming the
next declaration. The function is Apache-2.0 under its file header.

https://github.com/JetBrains/intellij-community/blob/840d3be11dfdeada533d05052008b48816b6cc4e/java/java-syntax/src/com/intellij/java/syntax/parser/StatementParser.kt

`Project` name binding is an original index over this parser's trees. One
rule comes from `ResolveClassUtil.resolveClass` in the same tree: the walk's
result is a class only when the processor returned exactly one candidate.
More than one candidate stays ambiguous here, and zero stays unresolved.
`ClassResolverProcessor` itself is not translated.

https://github.com/JetBrains/intellij-community/blob/840d3be11dfdeada533d05052008b48816b6cc4e/java/java-psi-impl/src/com/intellij/psi/impl/source/resolve/ResolveClassUtil.java

That file states: "Copyright 2000-2024 JetBrains s.r.o. and contributors.
Use of this source code is governed by the Apache 2.0 license."

Compiled classes in that IntelliJ tree are not parsed by the sources above.
`StubBuildingVisitor` extends ASM `org.jetbrains.org.objectweb.asm.ClassVisitor`
and builds stubs; `ClsClassImpl` then implements the same `PsiClass` interface
as a source class. This crate does not copy ASM, `StubBuildingVisitor`, or
`ClsClassImpl`. It parses class files with the `cafebabe` crate, version
0.9.0, by Kartikaya Gupta, which is 0BSD and contains `forbid(unsafe_code)`:

https://github.com/staktrace/cafebabe

`cafebabe` 0.9.0 depends on `bitflags` 1.3.2 (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and `cesu8`
1.1.0 (MIT OR Apache-2.0, Eric Kidd; some of `cesu8` is adapted from Rust's
`libcore` and keeps that project's license). No further transitive crates are
required. None of them invoke a JVM.

The rest of the lexer, parser, binder, and supertype walk is original. It is
not a translation of `JavaParser.kt`, the Pratt expression engine, IntelliJ's
scope processor, or `PsiClass.getSuperTypes`. It does not include IntelliJ
platform services (PSI, VirtualFile, ProgressManager, stub indexes, or the
compiler). No files from IntelliJ `java/java-tests/testData` are copied.
Fixtures under `tests/fixtures/` and the sources in `tests/bind.rs` and
`tests/classfile.rs` were written for this crate. `Lib.class` is a class file
assembled to JVMS §4 with major version 61. It was not produced by javac.
